      passage: `Dear Sofia,\n\nLast month, I moved to Britain to study English, and I want to tell you about some funny British customs I noticed.\n\nFirst, British people often say "sorry" even when it is not their fault. If someone bumps into them, they say sorry first! It is also very polite to say "please" and "thank you" all the time, even in small shops.\n\nBritish people usually stand in a line and wait for their turn; this is called "queuing", and it is very important to them. If you don't queue, people get angry, even if they don't say anything — they just look at you!\n\nThey also love talking about the weather. When two people meet, they often start the conversation with "Nice weather today, isn't it?" even when it is raining, because in Britain, it rains a lot.\n\nAnother British custom is drinking tea. Many people have a cup of tea in the morning, in the afternoon, and after dinner. My host family drinks about five cups a day! They usually add milk, which was strange for me at first, but now I like it too.\n\nOn Sundays, many families have a "Sunday roast" — meat, potatoes, and vegetables, all together on one plate. My host mother makes a delicious roast chicken every week.\n\nI am learning a lot about British culture, not just English grammar! I will tell you more soon.\n\nTake care,\nDiego`,
