      passage: `Sleep is often seen as wasted time, but scientists now understand that it is essential for both physical and mental health. During sleep, the brain processes information from the day, storing important memories and discarding unnecessary details. This is why students who sleep well after studying tend to remember information better than those who stay up late cramming. Sleep also allows the body to repair muscles, produce hormones, and strengthen the immune system, which explains why people who are sick often want to sleep more. Chronic lack of sleep has been linked to serious problems, including obesity, heart disease, and depression. Despite this, many people in modern society sleep far less than the recommended seven to nine hours, largely due to work pressure, screen use before bed, and busy lifestyles. Experts warn that treating sleep as a luxury, rather than a necessity, could have long-term consequences for public health.`,
