      title: 'Strange Burial',
      passage: `Burial customs around the world vary enormously, and some cultures have developed genuinely unusual practices for honouring the dead. In parts of South Korea, due to limited land availability, some families have their loved ones' ashes compressed into colourful beads instead of traditional urns, which are then displayed at home rather than buried. In Tibet, a practice called "sky burial" involves placing the deceased body on a mountaintop, allowing vultures to consume it, based on Buddhist beliefs about the impermanence of the physical body. Meanwhile, in Ghana, some families commission elaborate, custom-made "fantasy coffins", shaped like objects that represented the deceased person's life or profession, such as a fish-shaped coffin for a fisherman or an aeroplane-shaped coffin for someone who loved travelling. While these practices might seem unusual or even shocking to outsiders, they reflect deeply held cultural and spiritual beliefs about death, memory, and the meaning of a person's life.`,
