      passage: `Eco-terrorism refers to acts of violence or sabotage committed in the name of environmental protection, typically targeting property, corporations, or infrastructure believed to be causing significant environmental harm. Historically, groups associated with eco-terrorism have engaged in activities such as damaging logging equipment, sabotaging construction sites in protected forests, and, in more extreme cases, arson targeting facilities linked to environmentally destructive industries. Supporters of direct action argue that peaceful protest and legal channels have repeatedly failed to prevent serious environmental destruction, leaving more drastic measures as seemingly the only remaining option to protect vulnerable ecosystems. Critics, however, strongly condemn these tactics, arguing that violence and destruction ultimately undermine the broader environmental movement's credibility and legitimacy, potentially turning public opinion against otherwise sympathetic causes. Law enforcement agencies in several countries classify certain eco-terrorist activities as domestic terrorism, resulting in significantly harsher legal penalties compared to standard environmental activism or peaceful civil disobedience.`,
