      title: 'Should Motherhood Define Me?',
      passage: `Since becoming a mother three years ago, Rachel has increasingly struggled with a question many new parents quietly grapple with: how much should motherhood define her identity? Before having children, Rachel was a passionate marketing professional with clear career ambitions, but she now finds that most conversations, and much of her own self-perception, revolve almost entirely around her role as a parent. While she deeply loves her child and doesn't regret her decision to become a mother, she admits to occasionally mourning the loss of other parts of her identity that once felt equally important. Psychologists note that this experience, sometimes called "matrescence", the psychological transition into motherhood, is remarkably common yet rarely openly discussed, leaving many mothers feeling isolated or guilty for having such thoughts. Increasingly, parenting experts encourage new mothers to consciously maintain hobbies, friendships, and career interests outside of parenting, arguing that a fuller, more balanced sense of identity ultimately benefits both the parent and the child in the long run.`,
