      title: 'Coolcationing: A New Travel Trend',
      passage: `As summer temperatures continue to climb across southern Europe, an increasing number of travellers are rethinking their traditional holiday destinations in favour of a growing trend known as "coolcationing", deliberately choosing cooler locations to avoid extreme, often uncomfortable, summer heat. Countries such as Norway, Iceland, and Scotland have reported significant increases in summer tourism, with many visitors specifically citing rising temperatures elsewhere as their primary motivation for choosing these destinations instead. Travel companies have quickly capitalized on this shift, actively marketing northern European destinations as refreshing alternatives to the increasingly sweltering Mediterranean coastline. Beyond simple comfort, some travellers also cite genuine environmental concerns, expressing discomfort at contributing to tourism in regions already visibly struggling with the effects of climate change, such as water shortages and wildfires. While coolcationing remains a relatively niche trend overall, industry analysts predict it will likely continue growing steadily as climate change increasingly disrupts, and reshapes, traditional global travel patterns in the coming years.`,
