      title: 'Finding Calm in Cities',
      passage: `Urban living, while offering countless conveniences and opportunities, often comes at a considerable cost to mental wellbeing, with constant noise, overwhelming crowds, and relentless visual stimulation contributing significantly to chronic stress among city dwellers. In response, urban planners and architects worldwide are increasingly incorporating deliberate elements of tranquillity into city design, recognizing that access to calm, restorative spaces is not simply a luxury but a genuine public health necessity. Pocket parks, small green spaces tucked between buildings, have proliferated in cities like Tokyo and New York, offering brief but meaningful respite from urban intensity. Some cities have also introduced designated "quiet zones", areas where loud commercial activity and traffic noise are actively restricted, allowing residents momentary escape from constant sensory overload. Research consistently demonstrates that even brief exposure to natural elements, such as trees, water features, or simply unobstructed sky views, can measurably reduce cortisol levels and improve overall mood, reinforcing the argument that thoughtful urban design should prioritize genuine psychological wellbeing alongside purely economic and practical efficiency.`,
