      title: 'Second-Hand Fashion',
      passage: `The second-hand clothing market has experienced remarkable growth in recent years, driven by a combination of rising environmental awareness, escalating living costs, and the increasing acceptance, even fashionability, of pre-owned garments among younger generations. Online resale platforms have transformed what was once considered a niche activity, primarily associated with charity shops, into a sophisticated, multi-billion-dollar global industry, complete with dedicated apps, curated boutiques, and even luxury resale marketplaces specializing in designer items. Environmental advocates strongly champion this shift, pointing to the fashion industry's notoriously significant carbon footprint and highlighting how extending a garment's usable lifespan through resale can substantially reduce both textile waste and the demand for new, resource-intensive production. However, some industry critics caution that the second-hand market's rapid growth could paradoxically encourage even greater overall consumption, as buyers may feel less guilt purchasing items secondhand, potentially undermining the genuine sustainability benefits the trend claims to offer. Despite this ongoing debate, most analysts agree that second-hand fashion represents a permanent, structural shift rather than merely a temporary trend.`,
