      title: 'Personality and Health',
      passage: `A growing body of psychological research suggests that certain personality traits may be meaningfully linked to physical health outcomes, adding an intriguing new dimension to our broader understanding of what genuinely contributes to overall wellbeing and longevity. Studies focusing on the "Big Five" personality traits, openness, conscientiousness, extraversion, agreeableness, and neuroticism, have found that conscientiousness, characterized by traits such as self-discipline, careful organization, and reliability, consistently correlates with better health outcomes and, notably, even greater longevity across multiple large-scale studies. Researchers theorize this connection likely occurs because conscientious individuals are statistically more likely to consistently follow medical advice, maintain healthier daily routines, and generally avoid unnecessarily risky behaviours compared to their less conscientious counterparts. Conversely, high levels of neuroticism, characterized by frequent anxiety and persistent emotional instability, have been consistently associated with elevated stress hormone levels and increased susceptibility to various stress-related illnesses over time. While personality certainly isn't the sole determinant of health by any means, researchers increasingly argue that understanding these established psychological patterns could meaningfully help healthcare providers develop more genuinely personalized, effective prevention strategies for their patients.`,
