      title: '10 Things You Should Never Do When Starting a New Job',
      passage: `Starting a new job inevitably brings both genuine excitement and understandable nervousness, but certain common mistakes can seriously undermine an otherwise promising first impression. Career advisors consistently warn against arriving late during the crucial first week, as punctuality is widely regarded as a fundamental indicator of genuine reliability and professionalism. Similarly, excessive complaining about your previous employer, however genuinely justified your grievances might actually be, can create a distinctly negative perception among new colleagues who barely know you yet. New employees should also studiously avoid dominating conversations or aggressively offering unsolicited opinions before they have taken adequate time to properly understand established team dynamics and existing workplace culture. Overpromising on deliverables, driven by an understandable desire to impress, frequently backfires when unrealistic expectations subsequently prove impossible to meet. Perhaps counterintuitively, asking too few questions can also prove genuinely problematic, since new employees who never seek necessary clarification often make entirely preventable, sometimes costly, errors. Career experts consistently emphasize that the first ninety days in a new role are disproportionately important for establishing a lasting professional reputation, making early, thoughtful choices particularly consequential for long-term career success.`,
