      title: 'Are We Becoming Dumber?',
      passage: `A provocative and increasingly contentious question has emerged within cognitive science circles in recent years: is human intelligence, or at least certain measurable aspects of it, actually declining across successive generations, despite unprecedented advances in education, nutrition, and access to information? This counterintuitive concern stems partly from what researchers term the "reverse Flynn effect", a puzzling phenomenon whereby average IQ scores, which had steadily and consistently risen throughout most of the twentieth century, appear to have plateaued or even modestly declined in several developed nations since roughly the 1990s. Proposed explanations for this troubling trend remain fiercely, often heatedly, debated among specialists. Some researchers point toward pervasive environmental factors, including increased exposure to certain pollutants and demonstrably declining educational rigour in various school systems. Others controversially suggest that constant digital distraction and heavy reliance on external technological devices for previously internalized cognitive tasks, such as basic navigation, mental arithmetic, and even sustained memory retention, may be gradually eroding certain specific cognitive capacities, even while simultaneously and measurably enhancing others, such as rapid information-processing speed and sophisticated multitasking ability. Critically, however, numerous prominent skeptics vehemently caution against drawing overly simplistic, alarmist conclusions, persuasively arguing that traditional IQ tests may simply be inadequately calibrated to properly capture genuinely new, evolving forms of intelligence that are increasingly relevant and valuable in our rapidly changing digital age, meaning the very premise of measurable cognitive decline itself remains scientifically contested and far from definitively established.`,
