  'It seems intuitive that having more options should make us happier: a wider range of products, careers, or holiday destinations ought to allow us to find something closer to our ideal. Yet a growing body of psychological research suggests that excessive choice can have the opposite effect, leaving people less satisfied with the decisions they eventually make.\n\nOne explanation lies in the cognitive burden of comparison. When confronted with dozens of near-identical options, people must expend considerable mental effort weighing subtle differences, a process that can provoke anxiety rather than confidence. Even after a decision is made, the sheer number of alternatives foregone tends to fuel regret, as individuals imagine that one of the rejected options might have been superior.\n\nThis phenomenon, often termed the paradox of choice, has practical implications well beyond consumer behaviour. Employers designing benefits packages, policymakers structuring retirement plans, and even restaurateurs compiling menus have begun to apply findings from this research, deliberately curating a smaller, carefully selected set of options rather than presenting an exhaustive list. Proponents argue that thoughtful curation, rather than sheer abundance, ultimately serves the end user best. Critics counter that reducing choice risks paternalism, denying individuals the autonomy to decide what matters to them. The debate remains unresolved, but the underlying research has undeniably reshaped how many institutions think about the architecture of decision-making.';

